The Start node is the entry point of a workflow. Every workflow must have exactly one Start node.

## Purpose
- Marks the execution starting point of the workflow
- Receives initial input parameters passed from outside
- Serves as the first step of flow orchestration

## Parameters
- **outputs.properties**: declares the workflow's input parameter schema (field name, type, default) — note the field is called outputs (the start node "outputs" these params for downstream refs)

## How to configure
Define each input parameter's type/default in outputs.properties. Downstream nodes reference them via `{"type":"ref","content":["start_0","field"]}`. **start without outputs = downstream gets nothing** (rejected at commit/test-run).

## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "start_0",
  "type": "start",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 180, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "Start Node",
    "outputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "query": { "type": "string", "default": "Hello Flow." }
      },
      "required": []
    }
  }
}
```
Field notes: `outputs.properties` declares the workflow input parameter schema; `default` is the parameter default value. Downstream nodes reference via `ref` with `["start_0", "query"]`.

The End node marks the execution terminal point of a workflow and is used to output the final result.

## Purpose
- Terminates workflow execution
- Returns the final output of the workflow
- A workflow can have multiple End nodes (in conditional branch scenarios)

## Parameters
- **inputsValues**: mapping of output field -> value (usually a ref to upstream: `{"result":{"type":"ref","content":["llm_1","result"]}}`)
- **inputs.properties**: type declarations (schema) for those output fields

## How to configure
The workflow's final output = the fields declared in the end node's inputsValues. **end without inputsValues = workflow produces nothing** (rejected at commit/test-run). To surface a condition's boolean: `{"result":{"type":"ref","content":["condition_0","if_match"]}}` (if_match is the conditions[].key). Note it is inputsValues (input refs), NOT outputs.

## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "end_0",
  "type": "end",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 800, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "End Node",
    "inputsValues": {
      "output": {
        "type": "ref",
        "content": ["start_0", "query"],
        "extra": { "index": 0 }
      }
    },
    "inputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "output": { "type": "string" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Field notes: `inputsValues` with `type: "ref"` references upstream node output; `content: ["node_id", "field_name"]` is the reference path; `type: "constant"` is a constant value.

The HTTP node is used to initiate HTTP/HTTPS requests and integrate external APIs or services.

## Purpose
- Calls RESTful APIs
- Fetches external data
- Triggers external system operations

## Parameters
- method: HTTP method (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE/PATCH)
- url: Request URL, supports variable references
- headers: Request header key-value pairs
- params: URL query parameters
- body: Request body (used by POST/PUT)
- timeout: Request timeout (milliseconds)

## Configuration
Configure method, url, headers, params, and body in the HTTP node. All fields support variable references, e.g. the url can be https://api.example.com/${start.input.id}. After execution, it outputs status (status code), response (response body), and headers (response headers).

## Notes
- Set a reasonable timeout to prevent long blocking
- Sensitive information (such as API Key) is recommended to be placed in headers
- Return values need to be parsed as JSON or text
## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "http_0",
  "type": "http",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 500, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "HTTP Request",
    "api": {
      "method": "GET",
      "url": { "type": "constant", "content": "https://httpbin.org/get" }
    },
    "headersValues": {
      "X-Test": { "type": "constant", "content": "gaia" }
    },
    "paramsValues": {
      "q": { "type": "constant", "content": "hello" }
    },
    "body": { "bodyType": "none" },
    "timeout": { "timeout": 5000, "retryTimes": 0 },
    "outputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "body": { "type": "string" },
        "headers": { "type": "object" },
        "statusCode": { "type": "number" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Field notes: `api.method` is the HTTP method; `api.url` supports `ref`; `headersValues`/`paramsValues` are headers/query params; `body.bodyType` supports `none`/`json`/`form`; outputs `body`/`headers`/`statusCode`.

The Code node is used to execute custom code logic and supports multiple script engines such as Java, Groovy, and JavaScript.

## Purpose
- Implements complex business logic
- Data transformation and processing
- Algorithm computation

## Parameters
- language: Script language (java/groovy/javascript)
- code: Code content
- inputs: Input parameter list (variable name + reference expression)
- outputs: Output parameter list (variable name + type)

## Configuration
Write code in the Code node, receive upstream variables via inputs, and declare return values via outputs. The Java/Groovy engine dynamically compiles and executes the code. After execution, each output field is produced.

## Notes
- Java code must conform to dynamic compilation specifications
- Pay attention to code security and avoid executing untrusted code
- Complex logic is recommended to be split into multiple nodes for easier debugging
## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "code_0",
  "type": "code",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 500, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "Code Node",
    "script": {
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "var nameStr = name == null ? \"\" : name.toString();\nvar result = { greeting: \"Hello, \" + nameStr + \"!\", length: nameStr.length };\nresult;"
    },
    "inputsValues": {
      "name": { "type": "ref", "content": ["start_0", "query"] }
    },
    "outputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "greeting": { "type": "string" },
        "length": { "type": "number" }
      }
    },
    "inputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "name": { "type": "string" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Field notes: `script.language` supports `java`/`groovy`/`javascript`; `script.content` is the code; `inputsValues` declares input parameters and their references; `outputs.properties` declares output field schema.

## Multi-Language Code Examples

### JavaScript (GraalJS Engine)
Code is evaluated directly; the last expression's value is the return value. Input parameters are injected as top-level variables.
```javascript
var nameStr = name == null ? "" : name.toString();
var result = {
    greeting: "Hello, " + nameStr + "!",
    length: nameStr.length
};
result;
```

### Java (DynamicCompileNode)
Must provide a public no-arg constructor and an `execute(Map<String,Object>)` method returning a Map.
```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
public class ExampleCode {
    public Map<String, Object> execute(Map<String, Object> inputs) {
        Map<String, Object> result = new HashMap<>();
        Object name = inputs.get("name");
        String nameStr = name == null ? "" : name.toString();
        result.put("greeting", "Hello, " + nameStr + "!");
        result.put("length", nameStr.length());
        return result;
    }
}
```

### Groovy (GroovyClassLoader)
Must provide an `execute(Map)` method. Can use Groovy syntax sugar.
```groovy
class ExampleCode {
    Map<String, Object> execute(Map<String, Object> inputs) {
        def result = new HashMap<String, Object>()
        def name = inputs.get("name") ?: ""
        result.put("greeting", "Hello, ${name}!".toString())
        result.put("upper", name.toString().toUpperCase())
        return result
    }
}
```

The Condition node routes flow based on a single condition (if/else semantics).

## Purpose
- Two-way routing: matched goes through the condition's key port, unmatched goes through else
- Boolean result exposed directly (conditions[].key is the output field name)

## Parameters
- conditions: list of {key, value:{left, operator, right}}
  - key: condition name (also the output field name AND the edge port name, e.g. if_match) — required
  - left/right: operands, {type:"ref","content":[upstreamNodeId, field]} or {type:"constant","content":literal}
  - operator: contains / not_contains / eq / ne / gt / lt / ge / le / is_empty / is_not_empty / is_true / is_false

## Edge rules (violation = guaranteed failure)
- **One edge per conditions[].key (fromPort=key) plus one else edge (fromPort="else")** — both required
- **Never write true/false as fromPort** — unmatched port means that branch's downstream never executes and the workflow produces nothing
- Both branches may point to the same end node: the end reads the boolean via inputsValues ref to the condition key

## JSON structure example
```json
{
  "id": "condition_0",
  "type": "condition",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 500, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "Contains check",
    "conditions": [
      {
        "key": "if_match",
        "value": {
          "left": { "type": "ref", "content": ["start_0", "inputText"] },
          "operator": "contains",
          "right": { "type": "constant", "content": "target" }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Edge examples
{"from":"condition_0","to":"end_0","fromPort":"if_match"}
{"from":"condition_0","to":"end_0","fromPort":"else"}

## Notes
- Single condition two-way: use condition; multiple mutually-exclusive groups: use branches (multi-condition does not exist as a type)
- Watch type matching (string vs number comparison)
- No matched port = downstream skipped, so the else edge must be connected

The Loop node is used to repeatedly execute a sub-flow, supporting modes such as fixed-count, list iteration, and conditional looping.

## Purpose
- Batch processing of list data
- Repeatedly executes a task until a condition is met
- Implements for/while loop semantics

## Parameters
- loopType: Loop type (count/forEach/while)
- count: Upper limit for fixed-count loops
- iterateOver: The list variable to iterate over
- condition: Termination condition for while loops
- Sub-flow: The node chain inside the loop node

## Configuration
Configure the loop type and parameters, and orchestrate the sub-flow inside the loop node. Each iteration can access the current element (forEach) or a counter (count). It can be combined with break/continue nodes to control the loop flow.

## Notes
- Avoid infinite loops; the while mode must ensure the condition is eventually unmet
- Pay attention to variable scope within the sub-flow
- Pay attention to performance when iterating over large lists
## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "loop_0",
  "type": "loop",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 540, "y": 0 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "Loop_1",
    "loopFor": { "type": "ref", "content": ["start_0", "items"] },
    "loopOutputs": {
      "processed": { "type": "ref", "content": ["code_in_loop", "upper"] }
    },
    "outputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "processed": { "type": "array", "items": { "type": "string" } }
      }
    }
  },
  "blocks": [
    { "id": "block_start_0", "type": "block-start", "meta": {...}, "data": {} },
    { "id": "block_end_0", "type": "block-end", "meta": {...}, "data": {} },
    { "id": "code_in_loop", "type": "code", "meta": {...}, "data": {...} }
  ],
  "edges": [
    { "sourceNodeID": "block_start_0", "targetNodeID": "code_in_loop" },
    { "sourceNodeID": "code_in_loop", "targetNodeID": "block_end_0" }
  ]
}
```
Field notes: `loopFor` references the array to iterate; `loopOutputs` declares loop outputs (collects child node outputs as array); `blocks` are child nodes inside the loop; `edges` are connections between child nodes; use `["__loop_current__", "value"]` to reference the current iteration element.

The String-Format node is used to generate strings based on templates and supports template engines such as Thymeleaf.

## Purpose
- Template rendering (emails, reports, prompt assembly)
- String concatenation
- Combined output of multiple variables

## Parameters
- template: Template string containing variable placeholders
- engine: Template engine (thymeleaf/plain)
- inputs: Variable binding list

## Configuration
Use placeholders in the template (e.g. ${var} or [[${var}]]) and bind variable values via inputs. After execution, the formatted field is output as the rendered string.

## Notes
- The template syntax must match the selected engine
- Pay attention to special character escaping
- Complex templates are recommended to be split into multiple nodes
## JSON Structure Example
```json
{
  "id": "string_format_0",
  "type": "string-format",
  "meta": { "position": { "x": 500, "y": 180 } },
  "data": {
    "title": "String Format",
    "inputsValues": {
      "name": { "type": "ref", "content": ["start_0", "query"] }
    },
    "script": {
      "language": "spel-standard",
      "content": "Hello, ${name}!"
    },
    "outputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "formatStringResult": { "type": "string" }
      }
    },
    "inputs": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "name": { "type": "string" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Field notes: `script.language` is `spel-standard` (Spring EL template); `script.content` uses `${var}` placeholders; output field is always `formatStringResult`.
